<h2>Where LED savings come from</h2>
<p class="lead">An LED upgrade saves money two ways: it draws a fraction of the power, and it largely ends the cycle of replacing burned-out halogen lamps.</p>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Energy</h3><p>A 4–5 W LED can replace a 20 W halogen lamp, cutting that fixture's energy use by roughly 75–80%.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Lamp replacements</h3><p>Halogen lamps often last a few thousand hours, so a system running every night may need lamps replaced every year or so.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Transformer load</h3><p>Lower wattage means the same transformer can power far more fixtures, making future additions easier.</p></div>
</div>
<h2>Retrofit lamps or new fixtures?</h2>
<p>Swapping halogen bulbs for LED retrofit lamps is the lowest-cost route, but it only makes sense if the fixtures, sockets and wiring are in good condition. Corroded sockets, cracked lenses and brittle wire are common in older systems around Spring, and they will keep causing failures no matter what lamp is inside. In those cases, integrated LED fixtures and a quick redesign often deliver a better result.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Check compatibility:</strong> some older magnetic transformers and timers behave poorly with very low LED loads. A professional upgrade includes checking the transformer, voltage at each fixture and color temperature consistency.</div>
<p>Read more in <a href="/halogen-to-led-landscape-lighting/">Should You Convert Halogen Landscape Lighting to LED?</a> or let Spring Landscape Lighting handle it through our <a href="/services/landscape-lighting-repair/">repairs &amp; upgrades service</a>. You can also compare running costs in the <a href="/tools/energy-cost-calculator/">energy cost calculator</a>.</p>
